        <h2>The Problem</h2>
        <p>Many travelers to Zanzibar face: concerns about malaria, safe beaches, turtle encounters. With 2,900 monthly searches for "zanzibar malaria" and competing narratives online, visitors need clear, evidence-based guidance from a licensed Zanzibar tour operator.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="problem-section">
        <h2>Malaria & Preventive Health</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Risk exists:</strong> All of Zanzibar has some malaria risk, but incidence is low in tourist areas</li>
          <li><strong>Consult your doctor:</strong> Start antimalarials 1–2 weeks before travel; continue 4 weeks after</li>
          <li><strong>Common options:</strong> Atovaquone-proguanil (Malarone), Doxycycline, Mefloquine — discuss side effects with your physician</li>
          <li><strong>Dhow Coast recommendation:</strong> Bring a traveler's medical kit with prescribed antimalarials, rehydration salts, and basic first aid</li>
        </ul>

      <section class="problem-section">
        <h2>Safe Swimming Beaches</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Nungwi:</strong> Generally safe — strong currents possible at high tide; swim near resort areas with lifeguard awareness</li>
          <li><strong>Kendwa:</strong> Calm waters all day (no tide issues); full-moon party beach — swim cautiously at night</li>
          <li><strong>Paje:</strong> Lagoon swimming safe at high tide; watch for sea urchins at low tide</li>
          <li><strong>Jambiani:</strong> Safe swimming; tides expose seaweed farms — watch your step</li>
          <li><strong>Stone Town / Changuu (Prison Island):</strong> Safe with guided snorkeling; currents can be strong</li>
        </ul>

      <section class="problem-section">
        <h2>Turtle Encounters & Nesting</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Best months:</strong> July–October (nesting season), January–March (hatching)</li>
          <li><strong>Locations:</strong> Nungwi turtle sanctuary, Ras Nungwi, Kiwengwa beaches</li>
          <li><strong>Ethical guidelines:</strong> No flash photography, stay 10 meters back, never touch nesting turtles</li>
          <li><strong>Dhow Coast policy:</strong> We only offer turtle experiences with licensed guides who follow turtle conservation guidelines</li>
        </ul>

      <section class="problem-section">
        <h2>Other Health Considerations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sun exposure:</strong> UV index 10–12 year-round — use SPF 50+, hat, sunglasses, reapply every 2 hours</li>
          <li><strong>Dehydration:</strong> Tropical climate, active excursions — drink 3+ liters water daily</li>
          <li><strong>Food & water:</strong> Bottled water only, fruits you peel yourself, thoroughly cooked seafood</li>
          <li><strong>Insect repellent:</strong> DEET 50%+ or picaridin; apply over sunscreen (sunscreen first)</li>
          <li><strong>Prescription kit:</strong> Include antidiarrheal (ciprofloxacin or azithromycin), oral rehydration salts, bandages, antiseptic cream</li>
        </ul>

